					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/boogey-man23" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2362395); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2362395&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605795036" alt="Boogey_Man23" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2362395)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/boogey-man23" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2362395); return false;" >Boogey_Man23</a></span> <span class="odate time_1581787502 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">15 Feb 2020 17:25</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4502094">
									

<p>I'd go further than tacked on and say that the AWCY connection felt out of character for the group. To my mind, AWCY are mainly defined by a culture of 'high art' - appreciation for classical styles and postmodern innovation, installations displayed in exclusive galleries, making a statement with their work. Working for an animated television series aimed at a youth audience feels very far from that scene. I think distilling things down to &quot;drawings = artwork = AWCY&quot; is an oversimplification, without consideration for the mediums or culture.</p>
<p>That it comes out of nowhere right at the end kind of compounds the feeling that the mention lacks much justification for being there at all.</p>

